CM ============================================================
CM  TM-SC-NEC-014: Screwdriver Antenna — 20m Reference Model
CM  Complete documentation model with pattern output
CM  Frequency sweep: 14.000–14.350 MHz (8 points, 0.050 MHz step)
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  SYSTEM DESCRIPTION
CM  ========================================================
CM  This is the primary reference model for the screwdriver
CM  antenna system. It documents the complete NEC-2 structure,
CM  all modeling assumptions, expected performance, and serves
CM  as the baseline against which field measurements should be
CM  compared.
CM
CM  Physical antenna: motor-driven variable-inductance whip.
CM  Commercial examples: Tarheel, Hustler, Hi-Q, Scorpion.
CM  Whip material: aluminum or stainless; tapered in practice
CM  but modeled as uniform radius for NEC-2 simplicity.
CM  Average radius: (0.008 + 0.004) / 2 = 0.006m (specified).
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  ELEMENT GEOMETRY
CM  ========================================================
CM  GW 1: Main whip element
CM    20 segments, z=0.000m to z=2.400m
CM    Radius: 0.006m (average, base taper to tip)
CM    Feed point: segment 1 (base), EX 0 1 1 0  1 0
CM
CM  GW 2-5: Four horizontal elevated radials
CM    Each: 4 segments, 2.4m length, at z=0.005m height
CM    Radius: 0.006m (same as whip)
CM    Orientation: +X, -X, +Y, -Y (90 deg spacing)
CM    Purpose: act as ground plane / counterpoise
CM    Note: elevated radials reduce ground loss vs buried radials
CM    in mobile-equivalent deployment scenarios.
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  GROUND MODEL
CM  ========================================================
CM  GN 2 0 0 0  13 0.005
CM  Type 2: Sommerfeld-Norton real ground (most accurate for NEC-2)
CM  Relative permittivity (eps_r): 13 — average soil
CM  Conductivity (sigma): 0.005 S/m — average soil
CM  This represents nominal "average ground" (ARRL Antenna Book).
CM  For comparison: poor ground = eps_r=3, sigma=0.001
CM               : good ground = eps_r=20, sigma=0.030
CM               : sea water  = eps_r=80, sigma=5.0
CM  Ground quality has significant effect on low-angle gain and
CM  on feedpoint impedance at HF — particularly at 80m and below.
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  LOADING COIL — 20m CONFIGURATION
CM  ========================================================
CM  Series RL load at segment 1 of GW1 (base of whip).
CM  This models the loading coil as a lumped series impedance.
CM  LD 0  tag  seg_min  seg_max  R  X
CM  LD 0  1    1        1        1.171  374.8
CM
CM  R = 1.171 ohm: resistive loss in loading coil
CM    = Xant / Q = 374.8 / 320 = 1.171 (Q=320 assumed at 14 MHz)
CM    Coil Q of 300-400 is typical for air-wound, large-diameter
CM    screwdriver coils at 14 MHz. Smaller coils or dirtier forms
CM    may have Q=150-250, increasing R proportionally.
CM
CM  X = 374.8 ohm: inductive reactance of loading coil
CM    Required to cancel capacitive reactance of 2.4m whip at 14 MHz.
CM    Equivalent inductance: L = X/(2*pi*f)
CM                             = 374.8 / (2*pi*14e6)
CM                             = 4.26 uH
CM    This is a moderate inductance for a screwdriver coil.
CM
CM  Capacitive reactance of unloaded 2.4m whip at 14 MHz (approx):
CM    Xc ~ -1/(2*pi*f*C_whip)
CM    C_whip for 2.4m element ~ 20-30 pF (depends on radius, height)
CM    Xc ~ -380 ohm at 14 MHz (model-derived; X=374.8 confirms this)
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  FEED POINT
CM  ========================================================
CM  EX 0  1  1  0  1  0
CM  Type 0: voltage source, 1V excitation
CM  Applied at GW1, segment 1 (base of whip)
CM  This is the standard NEC-2 series voltage source.
CM  In the physical antenna, feed is via a tap on the loading coil
CM  (impedance transformer). For modeling simplicity, the tap is
CM  treated as a direct base feed. Impedance transformation factor
CM  from coil tap to 50-ohm feedline is not modeled here.
CM  To model coil tap: adjust feed segment or use NT (network) card.
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  FREQUENCY SWEEP
CM  ========================================================
CM  FR 0 8 0 0  14.000  0.050
CM  8 frequencies: 14.000, 14.050, 14.100, 14.150,
CM                 14.200, 14.250, 14.300, 14.350 MHz
CM  Covers full 20m band (14.000-14.350 MHz, 350 kHz, US)
CM  CW/digital segment: 14.000-14.150 MHz
CM  Phone (SSB) segment: 14.150-14.350 MHz
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  RADIATION PATTERN OUTPUT
CM  ========================================================
CM  RP 0  37  73  1000  0  0  5  5
CM  Full 3D pattern: theta=0-180 (37 pts, 5 deg step)
CM                   phi=0-360 (73 pts, 5 deg step)
CM  INORM=1000: normalized gain output
CM  Pattern confirms omnidirectional azimuth response.
CM  Peak elevation angle expected near 20-30 deg at 14 MHz
CM  over average ground.
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  OPTIONAL VARIANT: ELEVATED + SLOPED RADIALS
CM  ========================================================
CM  Alternative radial configuration: radials elevated to 1m height
CM  at inner end, sloping down to ground level at outer end.
CM  This reduces ground loss and raises feedpoint impedance slightly,
CM  making 50-ohm match easier without a transformer.
CM
CM  To use this variant, replace GW 2-5 with:
CM  CM  GW 2  4  0 0 1.0   2.4 0 0.0   0.006   (sloped +X)
CM  CM  GW 3  4  0 0 1.0  -2.4 0 0.0   0.006   (sloped -X)
CM  CM  GW 4  4  0 0 1.0   0 2.4 0.0   0.006   (sloped +Y)
CM  CM  GW 5  4  0 0 1.0   0 -2.4 0.0  0.006   (sloped -Y)
CM  Remove GE 1 flag (or use GE 0) to avoid ground interaction check.
CM  Sloped radials: inner at (0,0,1.0), outer at (±2.4, 0/±2.4, 0.0)
CM  This variant is NOT active in the current model.
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  EXPECTED PERFORMANCE (20m, average ground)
CM  ========================================================
CM  Gain vs isotropic (dBi): ~ +0.0 to +1.5 dBi at peak elev.
CM  Gain vs dipole (dBd): ~ -2 to -3 dBd
CM  Feedpoint impedance: ~ 15-25 ohm resistive at resonance
CM  (Rrad ~ 15 ohm + Rcoil 1.17 ohm + ground loss)
CM  SWR into 50 ohm: ~ 2.5-3.5:1 (matching network or coil tap needed)
CM  2:1 SWR bandwidth: ~ 80-120 kHz (narrow for mobile antenna)
CM  Efficiency estimate: Rrad/(Rrad+Rcoil) ~ 15/(15+1.17) ~ 93%
CM  (Ground loss not included in this estimate; actual lower)
CM
CM  ========================================================
CM  REFERENCE DOCUMENTS
CM  ========================================================
CM  TM-SC-NEC-001 through TM-SC-NEC-013: per-band models
CM  ARRL Antenna Book: Chapter on mobile antennas
CM  W4RNL (L.B. Cebik) screwdriver antenna studies
CM  NEC-2 Manual (NOSC TD 116): GW, GE, GN, LD, EX, FR, RP cards
CM ============================================================
CE
GW 1  20  0 0 0       0 0 2.4     0.006
GW 2   4  0 0 0.005   2.4 0 0.005  0.006
GW 3   4  0 0 0.005  -2.4 0 0.005  0.006
GW 4   4  0 0 0.005   0 2.4 0.005  0.006
GW 5   4  0 0 0.005   0 -2.4 0.005 0.006
GE 1
GN 2 0 0 0  13 0.005
LD 0  1  1  1  1.171  374.8
EX 0  1  1  0  1  0
FR 0  8  0  0  14.000  0.050
RP 0  37  73  1000  0  0  5  5
